@page "/live-regions"
<PageTitle>Live regions · Blazor Accessibility Toolkit</PageTitle>

<div class="page-intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">Browser tests · Playwright</p>
    <h1>Live regions</h1>
    <p class="lead">
        Status messages such as "Changes saved" or "12 results" must be announced without moving focus. That only works
        when the message is written into a live region (<code>role="status"</code>, <code>role="alert"</code> or
        <code>aria-live</code>) that already exists. <code>LiveRegionMonitor</code> records every live-region change in
        the browser, and <code>AnalyzeAsync</code> reports patterns that commonly keep messages from being announced.
    </p>
</div>

<ul class="reasons">
    <li><strong>Inserted with content:</strong> a region added together with its text is often not announced.</li>
    <li><strong>Hidden updates:</strong> text changed in a hidden region is never announced.</li>
    <li><strong>Conflicting politeness:</strong> <code>aria-live</code> values that contradict <code>role="alert"</code> or <code>role="status"</code>.</li>
</ul>

<section aria-labelledby="toast">
    <h2 id="toast">A toast versus a status region</h2>
    <p>
        Save in both examples: the message appears either way, so a test that only looks for the text passes for both. The
        difference is whether the region existed before the text was added. In the first example, the toast is inserted
        with its text; in the second, the text is written into a <code>role="status"</code> paragraph that was rendered
        from the start.
    </p>
    <ExampleCard Title="A toast inserted with its message" Defect="true" Example="LiveRegionExamples.ToastLiveRegion" Sources="@(["ToastSaveButton.razor"])">
        <Stage><ToastSaveButton /></Stage>
    </ExampleCard>
    <ExampleCard Title="A status region that exists before the update" Example="LiveRegionExamples.SaveLiveRegion" Sources="@(["SaveButton.razor"])">
        <Stage><SaveButton /></Stage>
    </ExampleCard>
</section>

<div class="callout">
    <p>
        <strong>DOM evidence, not speech.</strong> The monitor does not emulate a screen reader and cannot prove that
        anything was spoken. Announcements depend on the screen reader, the browser, timing and settings. Keep testing
        with real assistive technologies, and record it in the <a href="manual-review">manual review</a>.
    </p>
</div>

<p class="next">Next: <a href="accessibility-tree">accessibility-tree snapshots</a>.</p>
